{
  "$comment": "Scan-input test vectors for the share-URL binding (docs/transport.md § Accepting links from any host). Each vector is a URL exactly as a QR scanner or link handler would receive it. expect \"document\": extraction succeeds and yields precisely `document`. expect \"reject\": extraction fails; `kind` names the failure in the error vocabulary docs/integration-guide.md tabulates, and `reason` says the same thing in the words of transport.md § Encoding. `kind` is what an implementation asserts against — `reason` is prose, and an implementation is free to word its own differently. The algorithm under test: parse the URL, require http(s), read the d query parameter, base64url-decode UTF-8-aware (translate -_ to +/, re-pad), then dispatch on the first decoded byte — '{' (0x7B) is JSON; a low nibble of 8 that passes zlib's (b0<<8|b1) % 31 header check is a zlib stream, inflated with the output bounded at 8192 bytes and rejected on overrun, never inflated first and measured after; any other byte is an unrecognized encoding. Never try one form and fall back to the other on error. Then enforce the 8192-byte cap on the JSON document, JSON.parse, require a JSON object carrying a coffeejson member, gate on the major component of that version, and require the envelope to carry a non-empty beans or recipes collection. Both payload forms appear below deliberately, so an implementation supporting only one fails this corpus. Consumed by tools/validate-fixtures.mjs; other implementations are encouraged to run the same corpus.",
